# KServe Raw Mode Deployment

This standalone directory contains all the manifests required to install **KServe configured for Raw Deployment Mode**, ensuring that InferenceServices deploy as standard Kubernetes Deployments/Services (no Knative or Istio required).

## Prerequisites

### cert-manager (cluster prerequisite)

KServe webhooks require [cert-manager](https://cert-manager.io/) for TLS certificate provisioning. It is **not bundled** with this package — install it on the cluster yourself before running the installer.

```bash
# Pinned stable release — check https://github.com/cert-manager/cert-manager/releases for latest
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/cert-manager/cert-manager/releases/download/v1.17.2/cert-manager.yaml
kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pods --all -n cert-manager --timeout=180s
```

Verify:
```bash
kubectl get crds | grep cert-manager.io
```

### python3 (3.7+) + PyYAML (5.1+) (host prerequisite for `install.sh`)

`install.sh` performs a structured YAML rewrite of namespace references (so any custom `KSERVE_NAMESPACE` is propagated to certificate dnsNames, webhook clientConfig, RoleBinding subjects, and cert-manager `inject-ca-from` annotations). This requires **Python 3.7 or later** with **PyYAML 5.1 or later** on the machine running the installer. (PyYAML 5.1 added the `sort_keys` kwarg used by the rewrite; older versions error with `TypeError: safe_dump_all() got an unexpected keyword argument 'sort_keys'` — common on RHEL 7 / CentOS 7's bundled Python 3.6 with PyYAML 3.13.)

```bash
# Verify (must print 5.1 or higher)
python3 -c 'import yaml; print(yaml.__version__)'

# Install or upgrade (any one of)
pip3 install --user --upgrade 'PyYAML>=5.1'
python3 -m pip install --user --upgrade 'PyYAML>=5.1'
brew install pyyaml          # macOS Homebrew — ships current version
```

The installer (`install.sh`) checks for both cert-manager and python3+PyYAML and exits with a clear error if either is missing.

## Directory Structure

> Numbering starts at `02` because slot `01` is reserved for cert-manager, which is now a cluster prerequisite (see above) rather than a bundled component.

1. `02-kserve-crds`: KServe Custom Resource Definitions.
2. `03-kserve-rbac`: KServe ClusterRoles and authentication bindings.
3. `04-kserve-core`: The KServe Controller Manager (patched with `"defaultDeploymentMode": "RawDeployment"` and the `selfsigned-issuer` for webhook TLS).
4. `05-kserve-runtimes`: Default Predictor Runtimes (Scikit-Learn, PyTorch, Triton, HuggingFace).
5. `06-sample-model`: Sample manifests for testing. The directory contains:
   - `sklearn-iris.yaml` — standard iris InferenceService (online `gs://` storage).
   - `iris-input.json` — payload for the curl-based test.
6. `install.sh`: An automated deployment script handling the webhook sleep sequences.

> **Project scope:** this build ships only the core `kserve-controller-manager` (InferenceService serving). The `llmisvc-controller-manager` (LLMInferenceService) and `kserve-localmodel-controller-manager` (LocalModelCache) shipped by upstream KServe v0.17 are **filtered out at extraction time** in `generate-kserve-raw.sh`. To re-introduce them, revert the filter additions in that script and regenerate.

## Installation
Ensure you are connected to a healthy Kubernetes cluster and that **cert-manager is installed** (see above).

### Choosing the KServe namespace

By default, KServe is installed into a namespace named `kserve`. To install into a custom-named namespace (for example `my-kserve`), set the `KSERVE_NAMESPACE` environment variable. The installer creates the namespace if it doesn't exist and rewrites every namespace reference in the bundled manifests to match.

### Fast Installation
Simply execute the installer script from this directory:
```bash
# Default — installs into "kserve"
./install.sh

# Or pick your own namespace name
KSERVE_NAMESPACE=my-kserve ./install.sh
```

### Manual Installation
If applying manually, you must follow this exact order and wait for the pods in the previous step to become `Ready` before proceeding. The bundled manifests have `namespace: kserve` baked in — for a different namespace name, run `./install.sh` (which performs a structured YAML rewrite covering certificate dnsNames, webhook clientConfig, RoleBinding subjects, and cert-manager `inject-ca-from` annotations — a plain `sed` pass is not sufficient).
```bash
kubectl apply --server-side -f 02-kserve-crds/kserve-crds.yaml
kubectl apply -f 03-kserve-rbac/kserve-rbac.yaml
kubectl apply --server-side -f 04-kserve-core/kserve-core.yaml

# WAIT 15+ SECONDS for Webhooks to register their IPs in Kubernetes!
kubectl apply --server-side -f 05-kserve-runtimes/kserve-cluster-resources.yaml
```

## Validation
To prove the cluster is working, deploy the sample model in your workload namespace (default `default`):
```bash
kubectl apply -n "${KSERVE_WORKLOAD_NS:-default}" -f 06-sample-model/sklearn-iris.yaml
```
Once `Ready`, test it directly without ingress routing:
```bash
kubectl port-forward -n "${KSERVE_WORKLOAD_NS:-default}" svc/sklearn-iris-predictor 8080:80 &
curl -s -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d @06-sample-model/iris-input.json http://localhost:8080/v1/models/sklearn-iris:predict
```
